/* Daily Medical Fact dataset imported from the user's 200-fact clinical-pearl file.
   Two different entries are selected deterministically at random for each local calendar day. */
const DAILY_FACTS = [{"category":"Neonatal & Newborn","title":"Jaundice in the First Day Is a Different Story","text":"Jaundice appearing in the first 24 hours is not the usual pattern of physiologic newborn jaundice. It can signal haemolysis, infection or another pathologic process, so it requires prompt bilirubin measurement and clinical assessment rather than watchful reassurance.","pearl":"The clock matters: record the baby’s age in hours, not simply “day one.”","sources":[{"label":"NICE — Jaundice in newborn babies under 28 days","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg98/chapter/Recommendations"}]},{"category":"Neonatal & Newborn","title":"A Yellow Baby Needs a Number, Not Just an Eyeball","text":"Visual assessment alone is not reliable for estimating bilirubin severity. Bilirubin should be measured objectively and interpreted against the infant’s age in hours, gestational age and neurotoxicity risk factors.","pearl":"Never decide that jaundice is “mild” from skin colour alone.","sources":[{"label":"AAP — Hyperbilirubinemia in Newborn Infants (2022)","url":"https://publications.aap.org/pediatrics/article/150/3/e2022058859/188726/Clinical-Practice-Guideline-Revision-Management-of"}]},{"category":"Neonatal & Newborn","title":"Direct Bilirubin Changes the Question","text":"A raised direct or conjugated bilirubin points toward cholestasis rather than uncomplicated physiologic jaundice. The direct fraction does not need to exceed 20% of total bilirubin to be concerning; trends and repeat measurement can sharpen the signal.","pearl":"Ask about stool colour and urine colour when jaundice persists.","sources":[{"label":"AAP — Hyperbilirubinemia in Newborn Infants (2022)","url":"https://publications.aap.org/pediatrics/article/150/3/e2022058859/188726/Clinical-Practice-Guideline-Revision-Management-of"}]},{"category":"Neonatal & Newborn","title":"Prolonged Jaundice Is Not Automatically Breast-Milk Jaundice","text":"Persistent jaundice can coexist with biliary atresia, urinary infection, haemolysis, hypothyroidism or metabolic disease. The recommended work-up includes total and direct bilirubin at defined ages, rather than assuming a benign breastfeeding explanation.","pearl":"Pale or chalky stools are a particularly important clue to cholestasis.","sources":[{"label":"AAP — Hyperbilirubinemia in Newborn Infants (2022)","url":"https://publications.aap.org/pediatrics/article/150/3/e2022058859/188726/Clinical-Practice-Guideline-Revision-Management-of"}]},{"category":"Neonatal & Newborn","title":"A Cold Baby Can Be a Septic Baby","text":"In infants younger than three months, hypothermia or temperature instability can be a sign of serious bacterial infection. Sepsis does not always announce itself with fever; a low temperature in an unwell young infant deserves the same disciplined assessment.","pearl":"In a young infant, “not febrile” does not mean “not infected.”","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile child","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_Child/"}]},{"category":"Neonatal & Newborn","title":"A Well-Looking Febrile Infant Still Has an Algorithm","text":"For well-appearing, term infants 8–60 days old with a documented fever, the AAP guideline separates evaluation by age bands and uses urine testing, blood culture and inflammatory markers to guide decisions. The pathway is age-specific; it is not interchangeable with the approach to older children.","pearl":"Age in days is a diagnostic variable, not background information.","sources":[{"label":"AAP — Febrile infants 8–60 days","url":"https://publications.aap.org/pediatrics/article/148/2/e2021052228/179783/"}]},{"category":"Neonatal & Newborn","title":"The Neonatal Fever Threshold Is Intentionally Low","text":"A febrile neonate may need blood, urine and cerebrospinal-fluid evaluation and empiric treatment even when the first examination looks reassuring. The exact pathway depends on corrected age, appearance and local protocol, but neonatal fever should never be handled as routine viral fever.","pearl":"Do not let a cheerful appearance overrule neonatal age-based risk.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile child","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_Child/"}]},{"category":"Neonatal & Newborn","title":"Poor Feeding Can Be the First Sign of Serious Newborn Illness","text":"A newborn who cannot feed, or who has stopped feeding well after previously feeding normally, may be showing an early sign of severe infection or systemic illness. Newborns often lack the dramatic localizing symptoms seen in older children.","pearl":"Ask whether feeding has changed from the baby’s own baseline.","sources":[{"label":"WHO — Newborn danger signs (clinical-care resource)","url":"https://platform.who.int/docs/default-source/mca-documents/policy-documents/operational-guidance/BLZ-CH-59-02-OPERATIONAL-GUIDANCE-2010-eng-Belize-IMCI-C-Manual-for-CHWs-Final.pdf"}]},{"category":"Neonatal & Newborn","title":"Newborn Respiratory Rate Is Worth Counting Properly","text":"Fast breathing in a newborn is a danger sign, but crying and agitation can distort the count. Observe the infant when settled and count a full minute; persistent tachypnoea should trigger assessment for respiratory disease, infection or cardiac disease.","pearl":"A measured respiratory rate beats “looks like breathing fast.”","sources":[{"label":"WHO — Newborn danger signs (clinical-care resource)","url":"https://platform.who.int/docs/default-source/mca-documents/policy-documents/operational-guidance/BLZ-CH-59-02-OPERATIONAL-GUIDANCE-2010-eng-Belize-IMCI-C-Manual-for-CHWs-Final.pdf"}]},{"category":"Neonatal & Newborn","title":"The Newborn May Be Sick Without a Loud Cry","text":"Lethargy, reduced spontaneous movement, poor suck or movement only after stimulation can be more revealing than crying. A quiet, unusually inactive newborn is not necessarily a comfortable newborn.","pearl":"Compare activity with the baby’s usual behaviour, not with an adult’s expectations.","sources":[{"label":"WHO — Newborn danger signs (clinical-care resource)","url":"https://platform.who.int/docs/default-source/mca-documents/policy-documents/operational-guidance/BLZ-CH-59-02-OPERATIONAL-GUIDANCE-2010-eng-Belize-IMCI-C-Manual-for-CHWs-Final.pdf"}]},{"category":"Neonatal & Newborn","title":"Neonatal HSV Can Hide Without Blisters","text":"Disseminated or central nervous system neonatal herpes may present as a sepsis-like illness, hepatitis, coagulopathy, seizures or encephalopathy. Vesicles are absent in a meaningful proportion of affected infants, and a negative maternal history does not reliably exclude exposure.","pearl":"A sepsis-like neonate with seizures or hepatitis should keep HSV on the differential.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile child","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_Child/"}]},{"category":"Neonatal & Newborn","title":"The Bilirubin Risk Is Not the Same for Every Newborn","text":"Gestational age, haemolysis, sepsis, albumin and clinical instability modify the risk of bilirubin neurotoxicity. That is why one bilirubin value cannot be interpreted using a universal cut-off detached from the infant’s age and risk profile.","pearl":"Read bilirubin on the correct age-specific treatment chart.","sources":[{"label":"AAP — Hyperbilirubinemia in Newborn Infants (2022)","url":"https://publications.aap.org/pediatrics/article/150/3/e2022058859/188726/Clinical-Practice-Guideline-Revision-Management-of"}]},{"category":"Neonatal & Newborn","title":"A Rapid Bilirubin Rise Is a Clue to Haemolysis","text":"A bilirubin trajectory that rises unusually quickly can suggest increased red-cell breakdown, including immune haemolysis or enzyme-related causes. Serial values and blood-group/haemolysis assessment can be more informative than a single isolated result.","pearl":"The slope of bilirubin can be as important as the value.","sources":[{"label":"AAP — Hyperbilirubinemia in Newborn Infants (2022)","url":"https://publications.aap.org/pediatrics/article/150/3/e2022058859/188726/Clinical-Practice-Guideline-Revision-Management-of"}]},{"category":"Neonatal & Newborn","title":"Back to Sleep Is Still the Safest Starting Position","text":"AAP safe-sleep guidance recommends placing infants supine for every sleep on a firm, flat, non-inclined surface. Side positioning is not a safer compromise, and soft bedding or pillows introduce avoidable suffocation hazards.","pearl":"Position matters for every nap, not only overnight sleep.","sources":[{"label":"AAP — Safe infant sleep recommendations","url":"https://publications.aap.org/pediatrics/article/150/1/e2022057990/188304/"}]},{"category":"Neonatal & Newborn","title":"Room-Sharing Is Not the Same as Bed-Sharing","text":"The recommended arrangement is for the infant to sleep in the caregivers’ room, close to the bed, but on a separate sleep surface designed for infants. This supports proximity while avoiding the hazards associated with an adult sleep surface.","pearl":"Think “same room, separate surface.”","sources":[{"label":"AAP — Safe infant sleep recommendations","url":"https://publications.aap.org/pediatrics/article/150/1/e2022057990/188304/"}]},{"category":"Neonatal & Newborn","title":"Breastfed Babies Do Not Need Extra Water in the First Six Months","text":"Exclusive breastfeeding means breast milk alone, without water or other drinks, even in hot weather; prescribed vitamin or medicine drops are exceptions. Breast milk supplies the fluid needs of a healthy infant during this period.","pearl":"Do not confuse thirst in adults with an infant’s hydration plan.","sources":[{"label":"WHO — Breastfeeding: questions and answers","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/breastfeeding"}]},{"category":"Neonatal & Newborn","title":"Six Months Is a Nutritional Turning Point","text":"Around six months, breast milk alone no longer meets every energy and nutrient requirement, so complementary foods should begin while breastfeeding continues. Texture, variety and meal frequency should progress with development rather than remaining purees indefinitely.","pearl":"Complementary feeding adds to breast milk; it does not abruptly replace it.","sources":[{"label":"WHO — Infant and young child feeding","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/infant-and-young-child-feeding"}]},{"category":"Neonatal & Newborn","title":"Wet Diapers Are a Practical Feeding Clue","text":"After milk supply is established, regular wet nappies together with appropriate weight trajectory and stool pattern can support the impression that intake is adequate. No single diaper count proves effective feeding, so combine output with observed latch and serial growth.","pearl":"Feeding assessment is a pattern, not one isolated sign.","sources":[{"label":"WHO — Breastfeeding: questions and answers","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/breastfeeding"}]},{"category":"Neonatal & Newborn","title":"A Viral Symptom Does Not Close the Neonatal Case","text":"An infant can have coryza or a positive respiratory viral test and still have a concurrent serious bacterial infection. In an unwell young infant, viral findings should not replace age-appropriate assessment.","pearl":"A positive viral swab is not a universal “all clear.”","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile child","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_Child/"}]},{"category":"Neonatal & Newborn","title":"A Neonate’s “Normal” Examination Can Change Quickly","text":"Young infants have limited physiologic reserve and may deteriorate over a short interval. Repeated assessment of feeding, breathing, perfusion, tone and temperature can reveal a changing trajectory that a one-time examination misses.","pearl":"In neonates, reassessment is part of diagnosis.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile child","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_Child/"}]},{"category":"Emergency & Resuscitation","title":"Shock Can Be Present Before Blood Pressure Falls","text":"Children can compensate for circulatory failure with tachycardia and peripheral vasoconstriction, preserving blood pressure until late. Hypotension in a child is therefore a late and ominous sign, not a reassuring threshold to wait for.","pearl":"Look at perfusion, pulses, mental state and urine output—not blood pressure alone.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile child","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_Child/"}]},{"category":"Emergency & Resuscitation","title":"A Fast Heart Rate Is a Signal, Not a Diagnosis","text":"Pain, fever, anxiety, dehydration, hypoxia and shock can all cause paediatric tachycardia. Its meaning comes from the whole picture and whether it persists after addressing reversible stressors.","pearl":"Trend the pulse after comfort and initial treatment; do not interpret it in isolation.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile child","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_Child/"}]},{"category":"Emergency & Resuscitation","title":"Capillary Refill Is Useful—but Not Magical","text":"Prolonged capillary refill can support impaired peripheral perfusion, yet temperature, lighting, pressure technique and the site tested affect the result. It should be interpreted with pulse quality, skin temperature, mental status and urine output.","pearl":"A normal refill time cannot single-handedly rule out shock.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile child","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_Child/"}]},{"category":"Emergency & Resuscitation","title":"The Child’s Behaviour May Outweigh the Fever Number","text":"The height of fever and its response to antipyretics do not reliably distinguish serious from minor illness. Appearance, interaction, hydration, breathing and circulation are more useful for judging how unwell a child is.","pearl":"Treat the child, not the thermometer reading.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile child","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_Child/"}]},{"category":"Emergency & Resuscitation","title":"A Parent’s “This Is Not My Child” Comment Is Clinical Data","text":"NICE advises clinicians to take a caregiver’s perception of fever seriously. Parents often recognize changes in interaction, cry, feeding or activity before those changes are obvious during a brief examination.","pearl":"Ask what is different from the child’s normal behaviour.","sources":[{"label":"NICE — Fever in under 5s","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/NG143/chapter/recommendations"}]},{"category":"Emergency & Resuscitation","title":"A Non-Blanching Rash Is a Pattern, Not a Diagnosis","text":"Petechiae and purpura do not fade under pressure, but they have infectious and non-infectious causes. A rapidly spreading rash or a non-blanching rash in an ill child requires urgent assessment for invasive infection.","pearl":"Assess the child’s condition and rash progression together.","sources":[{"label":"NICE — Meningitis and meningococcal disease (NG240)","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng240"}]},{"category":"Emergency & Resuscitation","title":"Meningococcal Disease Does Not Wait for a Perfect Rash","text":"A child with meningococcal disease may initially have nonspecific illness, lethargy, limb pain or abnormal skin colour. A classic purpuric rash can appear late, so absence of that rash should not reassure you when the child is deteriorating.","pearl":"Do not use “no rash” as a rule-out test.","sources":[{"label":"NICE — Meningitis and meningococcal disease (NG240)","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng240"}]},{"category":"Emergency & Resuscitation","title":"Anaphylaxis Can Happen Without Hives","text":"Skin findings are absent in a minority of anaphylaxis cases. Sudden airway, breathing or circulatory compromise after a likely trigger can establish the clinical concern even when the skin looks normal.","pearl":"Do not wait for urticaria before treating suspected anaphylaxis.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Anaphylaxis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Anaphylaxis/"}]},{"category":"Emergency & Resuscitation","title":"In Anaphylaxis, Adrenaline Comes Before the Allergy Extras","text":"Intramuscular adrenaline is the first-line emergency treatment; antihistamines do not reverse airway obstruction or shock. In a child with possible anaphylaxis and wheeze, the RCH guideline prioritises adrenaline before asthma medicines.","pearl":"Treat the life threat first; antihistamines are not a substitute.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Anaphylaxis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Anaphylaxis/"}]},{"category":"Emergency & Resuscitation","title":"A Child Who Feels Better Should Not Immediately Stand Up","text":"After anaphylaxis, sudden standing or walking can worsen circulatory collapse. Positioning is part of treatment: keep the child lying flat, or sitting with legs extended if breathing makes lying flat difficult.","pearl":"Do not let a recovering child walk to the bathroom or car.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Anaphylaxis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Anaphylaxis/"}]},{"category":"Emergency & Resuscitation","title":"The Quiet Child With Croup May Be More Unwell","text":"A child who becomes quiet, exhausted or less responsive after struggling with stridor may be tiring rather than improving. Reduced noise can reflect falling airflow, so reassess work of breathing, air entry and mental state.","pearl":"Less stridor is not always better stridor.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Croup","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Croup_Laryngotracheobronchitis/"}]},{"category":"Emergency & Resuscitation","title":"Stridor at Rest Changes the Croup Severity","text":"Barking cough alone is different from stridor while the child is calm. Stridor at rest, chest wall recession, agitation or fatigue signals more significant upper-airway obstruction and changes the urgency of management.","pearl":"Listen when the child is settled, not only while crying.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Croup","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Croup_Laryngotracheobronchitis/"}]},{"category":"Emergency & Resuscitation","title":"Nebulised Adrenaline Works Fast—but Its Effect Can Fade","text":"Nebulised adrenaline can temporarily improve significant croup symptoms, but children who receive it need observation because symptoms may recur as the effect wears off. The RCH guideline specifies a minimum observation period after its use.","pearl":"Improvement after a rescue treatment is not the same as safe discharge.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Croup","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Croup_Laryngotracheobronchitis/"}]},{"category":"Emergency & Resuscitation","title":"A Child With Vomiting and No Diarrhoea Needs a Wider Differential","text":"Gastroenteritis is common, but vomiting without diarrhoea should prompt consideration of diagnoses such as obstruction, appendicitis, urinary infection, intracranial disease or metabolic disturbance. The history and examination determine which alternatives matter most.","pearl":"Do not let the word “vomiting” automatically become “gastroenteritis.”","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile child","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_Child/"}]},{"category":"Emergency & Resuscitation","title":"Intermittent Lethargy Can Be an Abdominal Emergency","text":"Intussusception may present with episodic distress, pallor, vomiting or isolated unexplained lethargy. The classic bloody “currant jelly” stool is not required for the diagnosis and may occur late.","pearl":"In an infant with unexplained episodes of going pale or floppy, examine the abdomen and consider intussusception.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Intussusception","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Intussusception/"}]},{"category":"Emergency & Resuscitation","title":"A Painful Testicle Is a Time-Critical Diagnosis","text":"Testicular torsion threatens testicular viability, and delay increases the risk of loss. When torsion is suspected, urgent surgical review should not be postponed to obtain ultrasound or laboratory tests.","pearl":"A normal-looking child can still have a surgical emergency.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Acute scrotal pain or swelling","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Acute_Scrotal_Pain_or_Swelling/"}]},{"category":"Emergency & Resuscitation","title":"A Seizure Lasting Five Minutes Is a Treatment Threshold","text":"Ongoing convulsive seizures should be treated as time-critical once they reach five minutes, because prolonged seizures are less likely to stop spontaneously and can become harder to control.","pearl":"Start timing the seizure as soon as it begins.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile child","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_Child/"}]},{"category":"Emergency & Resuscitation","title":"A Simple Febrile Seizure Usually Does Not Need a Brain Scan","text":"In a neurologically recovered child with a simple febrile seizure, routine EEG and neuroimaging are generally low-yield and are not recommended solely because the seizure occurred. Investigations should instead be guided by the source of fever and atypical features.","pearl":"Investigate the child’s illness, not simply the frightening event.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile seizure","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_seizure/"}]},{"category":"Emergency & Resuscitation","title":"Analgesia Is Not a Diagnostic Luxury","text":"Appropriate pain relief should not be withheld merely because a child may need surgical assessment. Pain control improves examination and humane care; it does not remove the need for serial reassessment when the diagnosis remains uncertain.","pearl":"Treat pain while continuing to look for the cause.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile child","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_Child/"}]},{"category":"Emergency & Resuscitation","title":"A Child Can Be Seriously Ill Without a Dramatic Single Vital Sign","text":"No single vital sign reliably captures paediatric deterioration. The interaction among respiratory effort, circulation, consciousness, hydration and trajectory is what makes a child look well or unwell.","pearl":"Use trends and the complete clinical picture, not one “normal” number.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile child","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_Child/"}]},{"category":"Respiratory Medicine","title":"Bronchiolitis Is Usually a Clinical Diagnosis","text":"Typical bronchiolitis is diagnosed from the history and examination; routine blood tests and chest radiographs are generally unnecessary. Tests can create noise, expose infants to discomfort or radiation and encourage treatment of findings that do not change care.","pearl":"If the presentation is typical and the child is stable, start with bedside assessment—not a routine chest X-ray.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Bronchiolitis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Bronchiolitis/"}]},{"category":"Respiratory Medicine","title":"Bronchiolitis Often Gets Worse Before It Gets Better","text":"Work of breathing commonly peaks around days two to three after symptoms begin, then gradually improves. A family may report that the infant was “fine yesterday” and is more distressed today even though the illness is following its usual course.","pearl":"Ask which day of illness the infant is on; the timeline matters.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Bronchiolitis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Bronchiolitis/"}]},{"category":"Respiratory Medicine","title":"The Main Treatment for Bronchiolitis Is Supportive Care","text":"For uncomplicated bronchiolitis, the priorities are adequate oxygenation, hydration and gentle supportive care. Routine bronchodilators, corticosteroids and antibiotics do not treat the underlying viral process and should not be prescribed automatically.","pearl":"A treatment not given can be evidence-based care.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Bronchiolitis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Bronchiolitis/"}]},{"category":"Respiratory Medicine","title":"Prematurity Changes the Bronchiolitis Risk Calculation","text":"Infants with a history of prematurity or significant cardiopulmonary disease are more likely to deteriorate or require admission. The same respiratory signs may therefore carry a different risk in a medically vulnerable infant.","pearl":"Always add gestational age and comorbidities to the respiratory assessment.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Bronchiolitis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Bronchiolitis/"}]},{"category":"Respiratory Medicine","title":"Chest Indrawing Is More Than “Working Hard to Breathe”","text":"Lower chest wall indrawing in a child with cough or difficult breathing is a recognised sign used in pneumonia assessment, particularly in children under five. It reflects increased effort and should be interpreted alongside oxygenation and general condition.","pearl":"Look at the lower chest while the child breathes—not only listen to the lungs.","sources":[{"label":"WHO — Pneumonia in children","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/pneumonia"}]},{"category":"Respiratory Medicine","title":"Fast Breathing Can Be the Key Pneumonia Clue","text":"In young children, pneumonia may present with fast breathing even when auscultation is not dramatic. Age-specific respiratory-rate thresholds and the child’s clinical state matter; fever and crying can temporarily raise the rate.","pearl":"Count the rate when calm and interpret it by age.","sources":[{"label":"WHO — Pneumonia in children","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/pneumonia"}]},{"category":"Respiratory Medicine","title":"Wheeze Does Not Automatically Mean Asthma","text":"Wheeze is common in viral infections and bronchiolitis, especially in infants. A first wheezing episode in a baby under 12 months is more likely to be bronchiolitis than classic asthma and often does not respond to bronchodilators.","pearl":"Age and episode history are part of the diagnosis.","sources":[{"label":"WHO — Pneumonia in children","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/pneumonia"}]},{"category":"Respiratory Medicine","title":"A Silent Chest Can Be More Dangerous Than Loud Wheeze","text":"In severe asthma, markedly reduced airflow may produce little or no audible wheeze. A child who looks exhausted, has poor air entry or altered mental state may be approaching respiratory failure even if the chest sounds “quiet.”","pearl":"Do not use wheeze loudness as a severity score.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Acute asthma","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Acute_asthma/"}]},{"category":"Respiratory Medicine","title":"The Best Asthma Severity Markers Are Often What You See","text":"General appearance, mental state, activity and work of breathing are central to assessing acute asthma severity. Peak expiratory flow and wheeze intensity can be unreliable, particularly in younger children or those too distressed to perform the test.","pearl":"Watch the child speak, move and breathe before focusing on the monitor.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Acute asthma","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Acute_asthma/"}]},{"category":"Respiratory Medicine","title":"A Spacer Is Not a Second-Class Way to Give Inhaled Treatment","text":"A metered-dose inhaler with a spacer can deliver bronchodilator effectively and is often preferred to nebulisation in children who can use the device. It is portable, fast and avoids some of the practical disadvantages of nebulised treatment.","pearl":"Check inhaler-spacer technique before deciding a medicine “failed.”","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Acute asthma","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Acute_asthma/"}]},{"category":"Respiratory Medicine","title":"A Sudden Wheeze After a Trigger May Be Anaphylaxis","text":"Wheeze that begins abruptly after a food, medication or insect sting can be part of anaphylaxis rather than an isolated asthma flare. When the two diagnoses are both plausible, the time-critical anaphylaxis pathway takes priority.","pearl":"Ask what happened immediately before the breathing symptoms began.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Acute asthma","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Acute_asthma/"}]},{"category":"Respiratory Medicine","title":"Steroids Are About the Exacerbation, Not Every Wheeze","text":"Systemic corticosteroids are used early in moderate, severe or life-threatening asthma exacerbations, but that does not mean every episode of infant wheeze should receive steroids. Confirm the likely syndrome and severity before applying an asthma pathway.","pearl":"Do not transfer an asthma treatment plan automatically to bronchiolitis.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Acute asthma","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Acute_asthma/"}]},{"category":"Respiratory Medicine","title":"Persistent One-Sided Air Entry Deserves a Second Diagnosis","text":"Asymmetrical breath sounds can occur with mucus plugging, but persistent focal reduction should raise alternatives such as an inhaled foreign body or pneumothorax. A “known asthmatic” can still develop a different acute problem.","pearl":"If the examination stays asymmetric, rethink the label.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Acute asthma","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Acute_asthma/"}]},{"category":"Respiratory Medicine","title":"A Barking Cough Points to the Upper Airway","text":"The characteristic barking cough and inspiratory stridor of croup reflect inflammation around the larynx and subglottic airway. This differs anatomically from the lower-airway bronchospasm that produces typical asthma wheeze.","pearl":"First decide whether the sound is inspiratory stridor or expiratory wheeze.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Croup","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Croup_Laryngotracheobronchitis/"}]},{"category":"Respiratory Medicine","title":"Humidified Air Has Not Shown Reliable Benefit in Croup","text":"Despite its popularity, humidified air has not been shown to meaningfully change croup severity. Calm handling and appropriate evidence-based treatment matter more than elaborate mist setups.","pearl":"Avoid interventions that agitate a child with upper-airway obstruction.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Croup","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Croup_Laryngotracheobronchitis/"}]},{"category":"Respiratory Medicine","title":"Asthma and Bronchiolitis Need Different Treatment Logic","text":"WHO’s 2026 consolidated guidance treats asthma in children and adolescents separately from bronchiolitis in infants and young children. Similar symptoms—especially wheeze—do not mean the same disease mechanism or the same medication response.","pearl":"A shared symptom is not a shared diagnosis.","sources":[{"label":"WHO — Asthma and bronchiolitis guidelines (2026)","url":"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240122680"}]},{"category":"Respiratory Medicine","title":"Oxygen Saturation Is Important, but It Is Not the Whole Child","text":"Pulse oximetry helps identify hypoxaemia, but motion, poor perfusion, probe fit and skin-pigmentation-related measurement bias can affect readings. A borderline number should be checked against the child’s appearance and a reliable signal.","pearl":"Verify an unexpected saturation before acting on the number alone.","sources":[{"label":"WHO — Pneumonia in children","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/pneumonia"}]},{"category":"Respiratory Medicine","title":"A Baby Who Cannot Feed Because of Breathing Is Not “Just Congested”","text":"Bronchiolitis can make sucking and breathing compete, so reduced intake may be an early marker of clinically significant respiratory effort. Hydration and feeding tolerance are central to disposition decisions.","pearl":"Ask how much of the usual feed the infant can actually finish.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Bronchiolitis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Bronchiolitis/"}]},{"category":"Respiratory Medicine","title":"A Coughing Infant Who Has Apnoea Needs Urgent Assessment","text":"Very young infants with respiratory infection may present with pauses in breathing, poor feeding or temperature instability rather than the classic cough-and-fever picture. Age can change the presentation of respiratory illness.","pearl":"In a young infant, apnoea is a red flag even without a dramatic chest examination.","sources":[{"label":"WHO — Pneumonia in children","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/pneumonia"}]},{"category":"Respiratory Medicine","title":"Clubbing Is a Clue That the Story Is Not Simple Asthma","text":"Digital clubbing is not a typical feature of uncomplicated asthma. Its presence should prompt consideration of chronic suppurative lung disease, bronchiectasis, cystic fibrosis or other underlying pathology.","pearl":"A chronic physical sign can expose a wrong acute label.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Acute asthma","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Acute_asthma/"}]},{"category":"Infectious Disease & Prevention","title":"UTI Is a Common Hidden Source of Fever","text":"In young children, urinary tract infection may cause only fever, irritability, vomiting or poor feeding, with no complaint of dysuria. When fever has no clear focus, urine assessment is often one of the highest-yield next steps.","pearl":"A normal ear and chest examination do not make the urinary tract irrelevant.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Urinary tract infection","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Urinary_tract_infection/"}]},{"category":"Infectious Disease & Prevention","title":"A Urine Dipstick Screens; It Does Not Finish the Diagnosis","text":"Dipstick testing can help identify children who need further evaluation, but a positive culture interpreted with pyuria is used to confirm UTI in the relevant clinical context. Collection quality matters because contamination can mislead.","pearl":"Read urinalysis and culture together, not as competing tests.","sources":[{"label":"AAP — Urinary tract infection clinical practice guideline","url":"https://www.aap.org/en/patient-care/urinary-tract-infection-clinical-practice-guideline/"}]},{"category":"Infectious Disease & Prevention","title":"Infants Do Not Need to Tell You It Burns","text":"UTI symptoms in infants and toddlers are often nonspecific. Unexplained fever, poor feeding, vomiting, irritability or faltering growth can be the only clues.","pearl":"The younger the child, the less you can rely on classic urinary symptoms.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Urinary tract infection","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Urinary_tract_infection/"}]},{"category":"Infectious Disease & Prevention","title":"The Way You Collect Urine Changes the Meaning of the Culture","text":"A contaminated sample can create a false diagnosis and unnecessary antibiotics. Choose an age-appropriate collection method and interpret growth in the context of pyuria, symptoms and collection quality.","pearl":"Before trusting a culture, ask how the urine was obtained.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Urinary tract infection","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Urinary_tract_infection/"}]},{"category":"Infectious Disease & Prevention","title":"A Sore Throat With a Runny Nose Is Usually Not a Strep-Test Story","text":"Clear viral symptoms such as cough, rhinorrhoea, hoarseness, oral ulcers or conjunctivitis make group A streptococcal pharyngitis less likely; CDC guidance says these children do not need routine strep testing.","pearl":"Do not swab every red throat.","sources":[{"label":"CDC — Clinical guidance for Group A streptococcal pharyngitis","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/group-a-strep/hcp/clinical-guidance/strep-throat.html"}]},{"category":"Infectious Disease & Prevention","title":"You Cannot Reliably Diagnose Strep by Looking Alone","text":"When clear viral features are absent, clinical examination cannot reliably distinguish group A strep from viral pharyngitis. A rapid antigen test or throat culture is needed to establish the diagnosis.","pearl":"A convincing-looking throat is not microbiologic confirmation.","sources":[{"label":"CDC — Clinical guidance for Group A streptococcal pharyngitis","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/group-a-strep/hcp/clinical-guidance/strep-throat.html"}]},{"category":"Infectious Disease & Prevention","title":"A Negative Rapid Strep Test May Need a Backup Culture","text":"For symptomatic children aged three years and older, CDC recommends confirming a negative rapid antigen test with a throat culture. This catches cases that a rapid test may miss and helps avoid unnecessary empiric antibiotics.","pearl":"Know the age-specific follow-up rule for a negative test.","sources":[{"label":"CDC — Clinical guidance for Group A streptococcal pharyngitis","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/group-a-strep/hcp/clinical-guidance/strep-throat.html"}]},{"category":"Infectious Disease & Prevention","title":"Antibiotics for Viral Sore Throat Do Not Prevent Rheumatic Fever","text":"Antibiotics are indicated for confirmed group A streptococcal pharyngitis, not for a viral sore throat. Diagnostic discipline reduces adverse effects and resistance while preserving treatment for children who benefit.","pearl":"Treat the organism you have evidence for—not the redness you see.","sources":[{"label":"CDC — Clinical guidance for Group A streptococcal pharyngitis","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/group-a-strep/hcp/clinical-guidance/strep-throat.html"}]},{"category":"Infectious Disease & Prevention","title":"A Positive Viral Test Does Not Exclude a Second Infection","text":"In a child who appears unwell, a respiratory virus can coexist with a serious bacterial infection. The test result should update the differential, not end the assessment.","pearl":"The child’s clinical condition still has the final word.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile child","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_Child/"}]},{"category":"Infectious Disease & Prevention","title":"Kawasaki Disease Is a Fever Diagnosis as Much as a Rash Diagnosis","text":"Prolonged fever with conjunctival, oral, skin, extremity or lymph-node changes should prompt consideration of Kawasaki disease. Not every child displays the full textbook set of features.","pearl":"Look for the combination and the timeline, not one perfect sign.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Kawasaki disease","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Kawasaki_disease_guideline/"}]},{"category":"Infectious Disease & Prevention","title":"Infants Can Have Incomplete Kawasaki Disease","text":"Infants, especially those under 12 months, may have prolonged unexplained fever with fewer classic features and a higher risk of coronary complications. A partial presentation should not be dismissed solely because the full checklist is absent.","pearl":"In infants, a missing feature does not safely exclude Kawasaki disease.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Kawasaki disease","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Kawasaki_disease_guideline/"}]},{"category":"Infectious Disease & Prevention","title":"An Early Normal Echocardiogram Does Not Rule Out Kawasaki Disease","text":"Echocardiography can be normal early in the illness. Clinical suspicion and timely specialist-guided treatment should not be delayed while waiting for a scan to become abnormal.","pearl":"The echo supports diagnosis and follow-up; it does not replace clinical judgement.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Kawasaki disease","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Kawasaki_disease_guideline/"}]},{"category":"Infectious Disease & Prevention","title":"IgA Vasculitis Can Start With Abdominal or Joint Pain","text":"The classic palpable purpura may not be the first symptom. Abdominal pain, arthralgia or oedema can precede the recognisable rash by days, so the order of symptoms matters.","pearl":"A child with colicky abdominal pain and later leg purpura may have one systemic process.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — IgA vasculitis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/henochSchonlein_Purpura/"}]},{"category":"Infectious Disease & Prevention","title":"The Rash Can Resolve While the Kidney Risk Is Still Developing","text":"Renal involvement in IgA vasculitis may appear after the skin and joint symptoms. Blood pressure and urinalysis follow-up are therefore required over time, even when the child looks recovered.","pearl":"Discharge is not the end of renal surveillance.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — IgA vasculitis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/henochSchonlein_Purpura/"}]},{"category":"Infectious Disease & Prevention","title":"A Vaccine Schedule Is a Living Clinical Tool","text":"Schedules and catch-up intervals can change as recommendations evolve. Use the current national schedule and the child’s documented history rather than relying on a remembered timetable from medical school.","pearl":"Always check the latest local schedule before advising or administering vaccines.","sources":[{"label":"CDC — Child and adolescent immunization schedule","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/vaccines/hcp/imz-schedules/child-adolescent-age.html"}]},{"category":"Infectious Disease & Prevention","title":"A Missed Vaccine Is Usually a Catch-Up Problem, Not a Restart","text":"Many vaccine series do not need to be restarted after a delay; the appropriate interval and catch-up table determine the next dose. Exact decisions depend on the product, age and national schedule.","pearl":"Reconstruct the record before declaring a child “unvaccinated.”","sources":[{"label":"CDC — Child and adolescent immunization schedule","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/vaccines/hcp/imz-schedules/child-adolescent-age.html"}]},{"category":"Infectious Disease & Prevention","title":"IgA Vasculitis Is a Multisystem Disease, Not Just a Skin Rash","text":"The small-vessel vasculitis can involve skin, joints, bowel, kidneys and occasionally other organs. Severe abdominal pain, gastrointestinal bleeding, reduced urine or hypertension should trigger escalation.","pearl":"When the rash appears, examine beyond the skin.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — IgA vasculitis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/henochSchonlein_Purpura/"}]},{"category":"Infectious Disease & Prevention","title":"Penicillin Resistance Is Not the Same as Macrolide Resistance","text":"CDC notes that resistance to penicillin has not been reported in clinical group A strep isolates, whereas macrolide and clindamycin resistance varies geographically and over time. Local susceptibility patterns matter when choosing alternatives for allergy.","pearl":"Do not assume all “strep antibiotics” have the same resistance profile.","sources":[{"label":"CDC — Clinical guidance for Group A streptococcal pharyngitis","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/group-a-strep/hcp/clinical-guidance/strep-throat.html"}]},{"category":"Infectious Disease & Prevention","title":"Fever Duration Can Reveal a Diagnosis That Was Invisible on Day One","text":"Persistent fever should trigger a fresh differential rather than repeated relabelling as “viral.” Kawasaki disease, occult bacterial infection, inflammatory disease and other causes may become more recognisable as the timeline evolves.","pearl":"At every review, ask: what diagnosis becomes more likely because time has passed?","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile child","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_Child/"}]},{"category":"Infectious Disease & Prevention","title":"Vaccination Changes the Differential—but Does Not Erase It","text":"Immunisation reduces the likelihood of several invasive infections, but unvaccinated or incompletely vaccinated children carry different risks. Vaccination status should be part of the first-pass history in a febrile child.","pearl":"Ask for the record; do not rely only on “vaccines are up to date.”","sources":[{"label":"CDC — Child and adolescent immunization schedule","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/vaccines/hcp/imz-schedules/child-adolescent-age.html"}]},{"category":"Cardiology & Circulation","title":"A Murmur Plus Sweating During Feeds Is a Red Flag","text":"Infants with heart failure may show breathlessness, sweating, tiring or poor intake during feeding because feeding is exertion. A murmur in that context is not simply an incidental auscultation finding.","pearl":"Ask whether feeding makes the baby work, sweat or stop to breathe.","sources":[{"label":"NSW Health — Cardiac murmur in paediatric patients","url":"https://www.health.nsw.gov.au/outpatients/referrals/Pages/cardiac-murmur-paediatric.aspx"}]},{"category":"Cardiology & Circulation","title":"Femoral Pulses Are a Bedside Screen for Coarctation","text":"Weak or absent femoral pulses in a newborn can signal impaired flow through the aorta, including coarctation. Comparing upper- and lower-limb pulses is a simple examination step with potentially major consequences.","pearl":"Do not finish a newborn cardiovascular exam without checking femoral pulses.","sources":[{"label":"NSW Health — Cardiac murmur in paediatric patients","url":"https://www.health.nsw.gov.au/outpatients/referrals/Pages/cardiac-murmur-paediatric.aspx"}]},{"category":"Cardiology & Circulation","title":"A Low Foot Oxygen Saturation Can Be a Cardiac Clue","text":"A newly detected murmur with low post-ductal oxygen saturation, central cyanosis or poor feeding in a young infant warrants urgent cardiac assessment. Oxygen saturation adds information that the sound of the murmur cannot provide.","pearl":"Pair auscultation with pulses, feeding history and oxygen saturation.","sources":[{"label":"NSW Health — Cardiac murmur in paediatric patients","url":"https://www.health.nsw.gov.au/outpatients/referrals/Pages/cardiac-murmur-paediatric.aspx"}]},{"category":"Cardiology & Circulation","title":"Syncope During Exercise Is Not Typical Simple Fainting","text":"Fainting during exertion raises concern for a cardiac cause, including arrhythmia or structural disease. Syncope after exercise with a typical vasovagal prodrome is a different pattern, but exertional collapse requires urgent evaluation.","pearl":"Ask whether the child fainted while running or only after stopping.","sources":[{"label":"Stead Family Children’s Hospital — Pediatric syncope referral red flags","url":"https://uihc.org/childrens/educational-resources/managing-syncope-and-indications-pediatric-cardiology-referral"}]},{"category":"Cardiology & Circulation","title":"A Family History Can Be the Most Important “Test” in Syncope","text":"Sudden unexplained death, inherited cardiomyopathy, channelopathy, pacemaker or defibrillator at a young age in the family changes the risk assessment of a fainting child.","pearl":"Ask specifically about sudden deaths and unexplained seizures in relatives.","sources":[{"label":"Stead Family Children’s Hospital — Pediatric syncope referral red flags","url":"https://uihc.org/childrens/educational-resources/managing-syncope-and-indications-pediatric-cardiology-referral"}]},{"category":"Cardiology & Circulation","title":"Palpitations Before Fainting Point Toward the Heart","text":"A history of racing heartbeat or palpitations immediately before syncope is a red flag for a possible arrhythmic mechanism. The sequence of symptoms is often more informative than the word “dizziness.”","pearl":"Write down what happened first: palpitations, chest pain, dizziness or loss of consciousness.","sources":[{"label":"Stead Family Children’s Hospital — Pediatric syncope referral red flags","url":"https://uihc.org/childrens/educational-resources/managing-syncope-and-indications-pediatric-cardiology-referral"}]},{"category":"Cardiology & Circulation","title":"Poor Weight Gain Can Be a Cardiac Symptom","text":"An infant with increased cardiac work may burn energy during breathing and feeding while taking in less milk. Failure to thrive can therefore be a manifestation of cardiac disease, even without obvious cyanosis.","pearl":"Growth trajectory is part of the cardiovascular examination.","sources":[{"label":"NSW Health — Cardiac murmur in paediatric patients","url":"https://www.health.nsw.gov.au/outpatients/referrals/Pages/cardiac-murmur-paediatric.aspx"}]},{"category":"Cardiology & Circulation","title":"A Murmur Is Not the Whole Cardiac Examination","text":"The same murmur can have different significance depending on age, oxygenation, pulses, growth, respiratory effort and perfusion. A quiet murmur with red flags can matter more than a loud innocent-sounding one in a thriving child.","pearl":"Interpret the sound in the context of the child, not in isolation.","sources":[{"label":"NSW Health — Cardiac murmur in paediatric patients","url":"https://www.health.nsw.gov.au/outpatients/referrals/Pages/cardiac-murmur-paediatric.aspx"}]},{"category":"Cardiology & Circulation","title":"Cyanosis Around the Mouth Is Not Always Central Cyanosis","text":"Peripheral colour changes can occur with cold or vasoconstriction, while central cyanosis involves the tongue and mucous membranes and is more concerning for arterial desaturation. Examine the tongue and assess oxygenation rather than judging lips alone.","pearl":"Look at the tongue in good light when assessing central cyanosis.","sources":[{"label":"NSW Health — Cardiac murmur in paediatric patients","url":"https://www.health.nsw.gov.au/outpatients/referrals/Pages/cardiac-murmur-paediatric.aspx"}]},{"category":"Cardiology & Circulation","title":"A Normal Pulse Does Not Exclude a Structural Heart Problem","text":"Some congenital lesions may not cause obvious tachycardia or a dramatic murmur early in life. Feeding difficulty, weak pulses, differential saturation or poor growth can be the clues that bring the diagnosis into focus.","pearl":"A normal rate is only one piece of the cardiovascular picture.","sources":[{"label":"NSW Health — Cardiac murmur in paediatric patients","url":"https://www.health.nsw.gov.au/outpatients/referrals/Pages/cardiac-murmur-paediatric.aspx"}]},{"category":"Cardiology & Circulation","title":"Newborn Circulation Changes After Birth—So Timing Matters","text":"The transition from fetal to neonatal circulation changes shunt flow and systemic vascular resistance over the first hours and days. Some congenital heart lesions become more apparent as the ductus arteriosus constricts.","pearl":"A baby who deteriorates after an initially reassuring period needs a fresh cardiac assessment.","sources":[{"label":"NSW Health — Cardiac murmur in paediatric patients","url":"https://www.health.nsw.gov.au/outpatients/referrals/Pages/cardiac-murmur-paediatric.aspx"}]},{"category":"Cardiology & Circulation","title":"An ECG Is Part of the First-Line Syncope Story","text":"The history, examination and ECG help screen for cardiac causes of syncope. An abnormal ECG, exertional episode, palpitations or concerning family history should prompt paediatric cardiology assessment.","pearl":"Do not label recurrent syncope “vasovagal” without checking the red flags and ECG.","sources":[{"label":"Stead Family Children’s Hospital — Pediatric syncope referral red flags","url":"https://uihc.org/childrens/educational-resources/managing-syncope-and-indications-pediatric-cardiology-referral"}]},{"category":"Cardiology & Circulation","title":"Heart Failure in Infants Often Looks Like a Feeding Problem","text":"Tachypnoea, sweating, prolonged feeds, frequent pauses and poor weight gain can be the infant equivalent of exercise intolerance. Parents may describe feeding difficulty long before they describe breathlessness.","pearl":"In an infant, feeding is a stress test.","sources":[{"label":"NSW Health — Cardiac murmur in paediatric patients","url":"https://www.health.nsw.gov.au/outpatients/referrals/Pages/cardiac-murmur-paediatric.aspx"}]},{"category":"Cardiology & Circulation","title":"Blood Pressure Needs the Right Cuff to Mean Anything","text":"A cuff that is too small can overestimate blood pressure; one that is too large can underestimate it. Paediatric interpretation also depends on age, sex and height rather than adult cut-offs.","pearl":"A number without a correctly sized cuff is not a reliable measurement.","sources":[{"label":"NSW Health — Cardiac murmur in paediatric patients","url":"https://www.health.nsw.gov.au/outpatients/referrals/Pages/cardiac-murmur-paediatric.aspx"}]},{"category":"Cardiology & Circulation","title":"A Normal Early Echo Does Not Exclude Every Evolving Cardiac Problem","text":"Echocardiography answers a specific structural and functional question at a moment in time. If the clinical picture changes, repeat examination and specialist reassessment may be more important than relying indefinitely on an earlier normal report.","pearl":"Let the child’s trajectory trigger reassessment, not the old scan report.","sources":[{"label":"NSW Health — Cardiac murmur in paediatric patients","url":"https://www.health.nsw.gov.au/outpatients/referrals/Pages/cardiac-murmur-paediatric.aspx"}]},{"category":"Gastroenterology & Nutrition","title":"Vomiting Without Diarrhoea Should Make You Pause","text":"Although gastroenteritis is common, vomiting without diarrhoea should prompt consideration of other causes, especially when pain is severe, vomiting is bilious or the child appears unwell.","pearl":"Do not anchor on gastroenteritis before checking for obstruction and other causes.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Gastroenteritis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Gastroenteritis/"}]},{"category":"Gastroenterology & Nutrition","title":"Green Vomit Is a Surgical Warning Until Proven Otherwise","text":"Bilious vomiting can indicate intestinal obstruction, including malrotation with volvulus, and requires urgent assessment. In infants, green rather than simply yellow vomit is particularly important to clarify.","pearl":"Ask the caregiver to describe the colour; “vomit” is not enough detail.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Acute abdominal pain","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Abdominal_pain/"}]},{"category":"Gastroenterology & Nutrition","title":"Intussusception May Look Neurological at First","text":"An infant with intermittent pallor, lethargy or unexplained episodes of distress may have intussusception even without obvious abdominal pain. The bowel telescopes into itself, and symptoms can wax and wane.","pearl":"A child who suddenly becomes floppy between episodes deserves an abdominal differential.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Intussusception","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Intussusception/"}]},{"category":"Gastroenterology & Nutrition","title":"The Classic Intussusception Triad Is Not a Safe Screening Tool","text":"Intermittent pain, vomiting and bloody stool are often taught together, but not every child has all three. Waiting for blood in the stool can delay diagnosis.","pearl":"Suspect early from the pattern; do not wait for the textbook triad.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Intussusception","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Intussusception/"}]},{"category":"Gastroenterology & Nutrition","title":"Ultrasound Is the Key Imaging Test for Intussusception","text":"Experienced paediatric ultrasonography has very high sensitivity and specificity for intussusception. Point-of-care ultrasound may help confirm the diagnosis in trained hands, but should not be used alone to exclude it.","pearl":"A negative limited bedside scan is not a licence to ignore a convincing story.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Intussusception","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Intussusception/"}]},{"category":"Gastroenterology & Nutrition","title":"Resuscitation Comes Before Reduction","text":"Children with suspected intussusception can have fluid losses and circulatory compromise. Establishing access, correcting hypovolaemia and giving analgesia before sedation or reduction helps make the procedure safer.","pearl":"Stabilise the child before focusing on the image.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Intussusception","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Intussusception/"}]},{"category":"Gastroenterology & Nutrition","title":"Appendicitis Is Easier to Miss in Children Under Five","text":"Young children may present late, with vague symptoms, irritability, vomiting or diffuse pain rather than a clear story of migrating right-lower-quadrant pain. Their limited ability to localise pain increases the value of repeated examination.","pearl":"In preschool children, a soft early examination does not always settle the question.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Acute abdominal pain","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Abdominal_pain/"}]},{"category":"Gastroenterology & Nutrition","title":"A Scrotal Examination Belongs in the Abdominal Pain Work-Up","text":"Testicular torsion and incarcerated hernia can present with lower abdominal or groin pain. Missing the genital examination can send the clinician down the wrong diagnostic path while time is lost.","pearl":"When a boy has acute abdominal pain, ask about and examine the scrotum.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Acute abdominal pain","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Abdominal_pain/"}]},{"category":"Gastroenterology & Nutrition","title":"Oral Rehydration Is Usually the First Route to Try","text":"For most children with gastroenteritis who need rehydration, the enteral route—oral or nasogastric—is preferred when feasible. Intravenous fluid is important when enteral rehydration fails or the child is too unwell, but is not automatically the first choice.","pearl":"Try the gut when it is safe and workable; reserve IV therapy for the right indication.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Gastroenteritis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Gastroenteritis/"}]},{"category":"Gastroenterology & Nutrition","title":"Breastfeeding Should Continue During Gastroenteritis","text":"Breastfeeding can continue during diarrhoeal illness, often with more frequent feeds. Stopping breast milk removes a source of fluid and nutrition precisely when the infant needs it.","pearl":"Diarrhoea is not a reason to stop breastfeeding.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Gastroenteritis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Gastroenteritis/"}]},{"category":"Gastroenterology & Nutrition","title":"A Normal Diet Can Return After Rehydration","text":"Once the child is rehydrated, the usual age-appropriate diet can generally be restarted. Prolonged fasting or routine restrictive diets can delay return to normal intake without improving the illness.","pearl":"Rehydrate, then feed normally as tolerated.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Gastroenteritis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Gastroenteritis/"}]},{"category":"Gastroenterology & Nutrition","title":"Blood in the Stool Changes the Gastroenteritis Assessment","text":"Bloody diarrhoea, a systemically unwell child, severe abdominal pain or prolonged symptoms may require targeted investigations and a broader differential. Most uncomplicated watery diarrhoea does not need extensive testing.","pearl":"Use red flags to decide who needs testing—not the presence of diarrhoea alone.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Gastroenteritis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Gastroenteritis/"}]},{"category":"Gastroenterology & Nutrition","title":"Constipation Is Often a Pain–Withholding Cycle","text":"A painful stool can make a child withhold the next bowel movement, allowing stool to become harder and more painful. The cycle can persist even after the original trigger has passed.","pearl":"Treat the withholding cycle and make toileting comfortable, not punitive.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Constipation","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/constipation/"}]},{"category":"Gastroenterology & Nutrition","title":"A Footstool Can Be Part of Constipation Treatment","text":"A stable foot support helps a child adopt a more effective toileting posture, with knees above hips and the body leaning forward. Behavioural measures and a positive routine complement medical treatment.","pearl":"Small changes in toilet posture can support a larger bowel plan.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Constipation","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/constipation/"}]},{"category":"Gastroenterology & Nutrition","title":"Routine Imaging Is Not Needed for Typical Functional Constipation","text":"In a child with a typical history and examination, constipation is usually a clinical diagnosis and routine investigations are not required. Tests become more relevant when there are alarm features or failure to respond to a properly delivered plan.","pearl":"Do not order an abdominal X-ray simply to prove a history that already fits.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Constipation","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/constipation/"}]},{"category":"Gastroenterology & Nutrition","title":"Complementary Feeding Is Also About Texture and Variety","text":"Starting solids at around six months is only the beginning; food consistency, diversity and feeding frequency should advance with age and developmental ability. A baby kept on very thin purees for too long may miss opportunities to develop feeding skills.","pearl":"Think beyond “started solids”: ask what textures the child can manage now.","sources":[{"label":"WHO — Infant and young child feeding","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/infant-and-young-child-feeding"}]},{"category":"Gastroenterology & Nutrition","title":"Responsive Feeding Teaches the Child to Notice Hunger and Fullness","text":"WHO recommends recognising and responding to hunger and satiety cues rather than forcing a set quantity. Pressure and distraction can turn meals into a struggle and may interfere with self-regulation.","pearl":"Offer nutritious food; let the child’s cues guide how much is eaten.","sources":[{"label":"WHO — Infant and young child feeding","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/infant-and-young-child-feeding"}]},{"category":"Gastroenterology & Nutrition","title":"Malnutrition Can Hide Behind a Normal-Looking Weight","text":"Oedema can increase measured body weight and mask severe nutritional depletion. In a child with bilateral pitting oedema, nutritional assessment must not be reassured by the scale alone.","pearl":"Look for oedema and muscle/fat loss—not weight in isolation.","sources":[{"label":"WHO — Infant and young child feeding","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/infant-and-young-child-feeding"}]},{"category":"Gastroenterology & Nutrition","title":"Too Much Cow’s Milk Can Crowd Iron Out of the Diet","text":"Large volumes of cow’s milk can displace iron-rich foods and contribute to iron deficiency in toddlers. It is also not recommended as the main drink before 12 months; after that, intake should remain moderate.","pearl":"Ask how much milk the child drinks in 24 hours.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Iron deficiency","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Iron_deficiency/"}]},{"category":"Gastroenterology & Nutrition","title":"Pain Relief Does Not Have to Wait for the Diagnosis","text":"Appropriate analgesia should be provided while a child with acute abdominal pain is assessed. The clinician can continue serial examinations and surgical review without making the child endure avoidable pain.","pearl":"Do not use untreated pain as a diagnostic instrument.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Acute abdominal pain","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Abdominal_pain/"}]},{"category":"Neurology","title":"A Simple Febrile Seizure Is Not a Diagnosis of Epilepsy","text":"A simple febrile seizure occurs in the context of fever in an otherwise neurologically healthy child and does not, by itself, mean the child has epilepsy. Long-term antiseizure medication is not routinely recommended after an uncomplicated event.","pearl":"Explain the difference between a fever-associated seizure and epilepsy.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile seizure","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_seizure/"}]},{"category":"Neurology","title":"A Simple Febrile Seizure Usually Needs No EEG","text":"Routine EEG is not indicated after a typical simple febrile seizure because it does not reliably predict recurrence or future epilepsy in that setting. Atypical features and the child’s baseline neurological status change the decision.","pearl":"Do not order EEG just because the event looked dramatic.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile seizure","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_seizure/"}]},{"category":"Neurology","title":"Recovery Time Is a Diagnostic Clue","text":"Persistent altered mental status after a seizure is a red flag, especially when recovery is delayed beyond the expected postictal period. Consider ongoing seizure activity, metabolic disturbance, CNS infection or another neurological cause.","pearl":"Document when the child returned to their usual level of alertness.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile seizure","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_seizure/"}]},{"category":"Neurology","title":"Focal Features Make a “Febrile Seizure” Less Simple","text":"A focal onset, prolonged episode or focal neurological deficit moves the event away from the simple febrile seizure category. These features call for targeted assessment rather than automatic reassurance.","pearl":"Ask which side moved first and whether both sides were involved.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile seizure","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_seizure/"}]},{"category":"Neurology","title":"A Headache That Wakes a Child From Sleep Is a Red Flag","text":"Night waking, early-morning headache, progressive worsening, vomiting or neurological symptoms can indicate a secondary cause such as raised intracranial pressure. They warrant timely assessment rather than reflexively labelling the pain as migraine.","pearl":"Ask when the headache occurs—not only how severe it feels.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Headache","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Headache/"}]},{"category":"Neurology","title":"Headache in a Child Under Four Deserves Special Attention","text":"NICE recommends urgent neurological assessment for children under four who present with headache. Young children cannot reliably describe pain quality, visual symptoms or progression, which makes careful observation especially important.","pearl":"Do not apply an adolescent headache template to a toddler.","sources":[{"label":"NICE — Suspected neurological conditions in children","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng127/chapter/recommendations-for-children-aged-under-16"}]},{"category":"Neurology","title":"Bending or Coughing That Worsens Headache Can Matter","text":"Headache triggered or aggravated by coughing, sneezing or bending down is among the red-flag patterns in children under 12. The positional or pressure-related nature of pain can point toward a secondary cause.","pearl":"Ask what makes the headache worse, not only what makes it better.","sources":[{"label":"NICE — Suspected neurological conditions in children","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng127/chapter/recommendations-for-children-aged-under-16"}]},{"category":"Neurology","title":"Most Childhood Headaches Do Not Need Brain Imaging","text":"Most children with headache have primary headache or a self-limited cause, and routine imaging is not needed when history and neurological examination are reassuring. Imaging is guided by red flags and examination, not by anxiety alone.","pearl":"A normal, carefully documented neurological exam is meaningful information.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Headache","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Headache/"}]},{"category":"Neurology","title":"Headache Plus Vomiting and Ataxia Is Not “Just a Migraine”","text":"Headache associated with vomiting, unsteadiness, altered consciousness or abnormal eye movements needs urgent assessment for a secondary neurological cause. The combination matters even if the child is temporarily comfortable between episodes.","pearl":"Look for gait and eye movement changes whenever headache is a concern.","sources":[{"label":"NICE — Suspected neurological conditions in children","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng127/chapter/recommendations-for-children-aged-under-16"}]},{"category":"Neurology","title":"A Child’s Migraine May Look Different From an Adult’s","text":"Paediatric migraine can be shorter, bilateral and accompanied by abdominal symptoms, nausea or pallor. A child may not describe the classic one-sided throbbing headache expected in adults.","pearl":"Ask about light sensitivity, nausea, activity avoidance and family history.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Headache","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Headache/"}]},{"category":"Neurology","title":"Antipyretics Do Not Reliably Prevent Febrile Seizures","text":"Reducing fever can improve comfort, but antipyretics do not reliably prevent another febrile seizure. Their role is symptom relief, not seizure prophylaxis.","pearl":"Do not promise parents that treating every fever will prevent seizures.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile seizure","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_seizure/"}]},{"category":"Neurology","title":"The First Aid Rule Is: Protect, Time, Position","text":"During a seizure, protect the child from nearby hazards, place them on their side when possible, time the event and do not restrain them or put anything in the mouth. Airway and breathing take priority over trying to stop the movements manually.","pearl":"Never put a spoon, finger or medicine into the mouth of a convulsing child.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile seizure","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_seizure/"}]},{"category":"Neurology","title":"A New Squint Can Be a Neurological Clue","text":"Headache with a new squint or failure of upward gaze is a warning feature in a child and can be associated with raised intracranial pressure. Eye movement and visual symptoms belong in the headache history and examination.","pearl":"Ask about double vision and look at extraocular movements.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Headache","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Headache/"}]},{"category":"Neurology","title":"A Seizure Can Be the First Sign of an Infection","text":"In infants and young children, meningitis or encephalitis may initially present with seizure, irritability, poor feeding or altered behaviour. The source of fever must still be assessed even when the seizure has stopped.","pearl":"A stopped seizure does not finish the infectious work-up.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Febrile seizure","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_seizure/"}]},{"category":"Neurology","title":"The Neurological Examination Should Include Walking","text":"Gait, coordination, balance, eye movements and mental status can reveal abnormalities that are missed by testing limb strength alone. Ataxia accompanying headache or vomiting is particularly concerning.","pearl":"Watch the child walk if it is safe and developmentally appropriate.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Headache","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Headache/"}]},{"category":"Renal & Urinary Medicine","title":"A “Recovered” Acute Kidney Injury Still Matters Later","text":"Children who experience acute kidney injury have increased risk of future kidney problems and recurrent AKI. Follow-up should include kidney function, blood pressure and urine protein assessment rather than assuming that a normal discharge creatinine ends the story.","pearl":"Record AKI in the long-term medical history and plan follow-up.","sources":[{"label":"Canadian Paediatric Society — Acute kidney injury in children","url":"https://cps.ca/en/documents/position/acute-kidney-injury"}]},{"category":"Renal & Urinary Medicine","title":"Dehydration and Nephrotoxic Medicines Make a Risky Pair","text":"During volume depletion, renal perfusion may fall; medicines that affect renal blood flow or directly injure the kidney can add to the risk. Medication review and hydration advice are important during significant illness.","pearl":"Ask about ibuprofen and other medicines when a dehydrated child has reduced urine output.","sources":[{"label":"Canadian Paediatric Society — Acute kidney injury in children","url":"https://cps.ca/en/documents/position/acute-kidney-injury"}]},{"category":"Renal & Urinary Medicine","title":"Creatinine Is Only One Part of Kidney Assessment","text":"Kidney injury is assessed through the clinical history, urine output, creatinine trend, electrolytes and underlying risk factors. A single creatinine value can be misleading without knowing the child’s baseline and size.","pearl":"Trend renal function and measure urine output where clinically indicated.","sources":[{"label":"Canadian Paediatric Society — Acute kidney injury in children","url":"https://cps.ca/en/documents/position/acute-kidney-injury"}]},{"category":"Renal & Urinary Medicine","title":"Blood Pressure Is a Kidney Test Too","text":"Hypertension can be a manifestation of renal disease and is easy to miss if blood pressure is not measured with an appropriate cuff. Kidney follow-up should include blood pressure, not only serum tests.","pearl":"Do not leave blood pressure out of a renal assessment.","sources":[{"label":"Canadian Paediatric Society — Acute kidney injury in children","url":"https://cps.ca/en/documents/position/acute-kidney-injury"}]},{"category":"Renal & Urinary Medicine","title":"A Recurrent UTI May Be a Bladder–Bowel Problem","text":"Constipation and bladder–bowel dysfunction can contribute to recurrent urinary infections in children. Asking about stool withholding, urgency, wetting and voiding habits may reveal a modifiable contributor.","pearl":"When UTIs recur, ask about the bowel as well as the bladder.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Urinary tract infection","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Urinary_tract_infection/"}]},{"category":"Renal & Urinary Medicine","title":"Not Every Child With UTI Needs an IV Antibiotic","text":"Oral antibiotics are appropriate for many children with UTI who are clinically stable and able to take medication. Age, sepsis, structural abnormalities and the ability to tolerate oral treatment determine when IV therapy is needed.","pearl":"Choose route by clinical status and age-specific guidance, not by the word “UTI.”","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Urinary tract infection","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Urinary_tract_infection/"}]},{"category":"Renal & Urinary Medicine","title":"Atypical UTI Can Change the Imaging Plan","text":"Severe illness, poor response, unusual organisms or recurrent infection can make a UTI atypical and increase the need for renal tract imaging. Imaging is not identical for every first uncomplicated infection.","pearl":"Ask whether this is a first typical UTI or an atypical/recurrent one.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Urinary tract infection","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Urinary_tract_infection/"}]},{"category":"Renal & Urinary Medicine","title":"IgA Vasculitis Can Affect the Kidneys After the Rash Fades","text":"Microscopic haematuria or proteinuria may develop after the purpura and joint pain have improved. Repeated urinalysis and blood-pressure monitoring are therefore part of follow-up.","pearl":"A clear skin examination does not prove that the kidneys are clear.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — IgA vasculitis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/henochSchonlein_Purpura/"}]},{"category":"Renal & Urinary Medicine","title":"Protein in Urine Is Best Interpreted With Timing and Context","text":"Transient proteinuria can occur with fever, exercise or illness. A first-morning urine sample can help distinguish persistent protein loss from postural or transient findings.","pearl":"Repeat an unexpected protein result when the child is well and use an appropriate sample.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — IgA vasculitis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/henochSchonlein_Purpura/"}]},{"category":"Renal & Urinary Medicine","title":"A Low Urine Output Is a Bedside Warning, Not Just a Chart Entry","text":"Reduced urine output can be an early clue to poor perfusion or kidney injury. Its significance depends on age, intake, fluid losses and duration, so the trend should be documented rather than guessed.","pearl":"Ask when the child last passed urine and compare with intake and losses.","sources":[{"label":"Canadian Paediatric Society — Acute kidney injury in children","url":"https://cps.ca/en/documents/position/acute-kidney-injury"}]},{"category":"Renal & Urinary Medicine","title":"A Bag Urine Culture Can Mislead You","text":"Non-invasive collection methods can be useful for screening in selected settings, but contamination makes a positive culture from a bag unreliable for confirming UTI. The method of collection must be considered before interpreting a result.","pearl":"Do not diagnose a UTI from a contaminated sample alone.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Urinary tract infection","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Urinary_tract_infection/"}]},{"category":"Renal & Urinary Medicine","title":"Medication Reconciliation Is Part of Kidney Protection","text":"After AKI, clinicians should identify avoidable nephrotoxic exposures and communicate the episode to future care providers. Families should know that dehydration and certain medications may create a repeat risk.","pearl":"A past AKI should change future medication and hydration conversations.","sources":[{"label":"Canadian Paediatric Society — Acute kidney injury in children","url":"https://cps.ca/en/documents/position/acute-kidney-injury"}]},{"category":"Renal & Urinary Medicine","title":"A Febrile UTI in a Young Infant Is Not a Minor Bladder Infection","text":"Young infants with UTI may have bacteraemia or structural urinary tract issues and often need a lower threshold for investigation and treatment. Age and general appearance change the risk.","pearl":"Use the infant’s age to set the threshold for escalation.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Urinary tract infection","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Urinary_tract_infection/"}]},{"category":"Renal & Urinary Medicine","title":"Blood Pressure Can Be the First Visible Sign of IgA Nephritis","text":"Renal involvement in IgA vasculitis may be clinically silent except for hypertension, haematuria or proteinuria. Routine blood-pressure measurement is a simple way to identify a complication that the child may not feel.","pearl":"Measure BP even when the child says they feel completely well.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — IgA vasculitis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/henochSchonlein_Purpura/"}]},{"category":"Renal & Urinary Medicine","title":"A Child’s Kidney Function Needs a Child-Sized Equation","text":"Estimating kidney function in children requires height- and age-appropriate paediatric equations rather than adult assumptions. The Canadian Paediatric Society recommends paediatric eGFR approaches in AKI follow-up.","pearl":"Use paediatric reference methods; adult eGFR shortcuts are not interchangeable.","sources":[{"label":"Canadian Paediatric Society — Acute kidney injury in children","url":"https://cps.ca/en/documents/position/acute-kidney-injury"}]},{"category":"Endocrine & Metabolism","title":"A Child With New Bedwetting May Have New Diabetes","text":"New polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss, fatigue or secondary enuresis can be early manifestations of diabetes. When these occur together, a bedside glucose check can prevent a missed or delayed diagnosis.","pearl":"Ask about thirst, urination and weight change in a child with new enuresis.","sources":[{"label":"NICE — Diabetes in children and young people","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng18/chapter/Recommendations"}]},{"category":"Endocrine & Metabolism","title":"DKA Can Masquerade as Gastroenteritis","text":"Vomiting, abdominal pain and dehydration are common in diabetic ketoacidosis and can look like a gastrointestinal illness. Deep or rapid breathing, weight loss, excessive thirst or altered consciousness should widen the differential immediately.","pearl":"Before labelling a vomiting child as gastroenteritis, consider checking glucose when symptoms fit.","sources":[{"label":"NICE — Diabetes in children and young people","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng18/chapter/Recommendations"}]},{"category":"Endocrine & Metabolism","title":"Known Diabetes Does Not Always Mean High Glucose During DKA","text":"Children taking insulin can develop ketoacidosis with normal or only modestly elevated blood glucose, including during illness or reduced intake. Blood ketones and acid–base status matter when symptoms suggest DKA.","pearl":"Do not use a normal glucose value to rule out ketosis in a symptomatic child with diabetes.","sources":[{"label":"NICE — Diabetes in children and young people","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng18/chapter/Recommendations"}]},{"category":"Endocrine & Metabolism","title":"In DKA, Blood Potassium Can Look Reassuring While Total Stores Are Depleted","text":"Insulin deficiency and acidosis shift potassium out of cells, so the measured serum potassium may be normal or high even though urinary losses have depleted total-body potassium. Treatment can reveal the deficit as potassium moves back into cells.","pearl":"Interpret potassium in the physiology of DKA, not as a simple measure of body stores.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Diabetic ketoacidosis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Diabetic_Ketoacidosis/"}]},{"category":"Endocrine & Metabolism","title":"Glucose Can Improve Before Ketones Have Cleared","text":"During DKA treatment, blood glucose may fall before ketoacidosis has resolved. Dextrose may need to be added while insulin continues so ketone clearance can proceed safely under close monitoring.","pearl":"A better glucose number does not mean DKA treatment is finished.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Diabetic ketoacidosis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Diabetic_Ketoacidosis/"}]},{"category":"Endocrine & Metabolism","title":"Bicarbonate Is Not a Routine DKA Fix","text":"Routine bicarbonate is not recommended in paediatric DKA because it can cause harm and does not correct the underlying insulin deficiency and ketogenesis. The RCH guideline reserves consideration for exceptional life-threatening circumstances with specialist input.","pearl":"Treat the mechanism—insulin deficiency and dehydration—not the pH number alone.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Diabetic ketoacidosis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Diabetic_Ketoacidosis/"}]},{"category":"Endocrine & Metabolism","title":"A New DKA Diagnosis Is a Specialist-Level Emergency","text":"Children with DKA require a setting capable of frequent biochemical monitoring and clinicians experienced in paediatric DKA. Very young age, severe acidosis, coma, seizures or cardiovascular compromise increase the need for intensive care.","pearl":"Arrange the right level of care early; do not manage DKA as routine dehydration.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Diabetic ketoacidosis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Diabetic_Ketoacidosis/"}]},{"category":"Endocrine & Metabolism","title":"Type 1 Diabetes Travels With Other Autoimmune Diseases","text":"Children with type 1 diabetes have increased risk of autoimmune thyroid disease and coeliac disease. The ADA recommends considering screening for these conditions, guided by symptoms, timing and clinical context.","pearl":"When diagnosing type 1 diabetes, remember the autoimmune family of conditions.","sources":[{"label":"ADA — Standards of Care in Diabetes, Children and Adolescents (2026)","url":"https://doi.org/10.2337/dc26-s014"}]},{"category":"Endocrine & Metabolism","title":"Insulin Omission Is a Common Trigger for DKA","text":"In a child with established type 1 diabetes, missed or substantially reduced insulin doses and intercurrent illness are common contributors to DKA. A careful, non-judgmental history often reveals the trigger and the prevention opportunity.","pearl":"Ask what happened to insulin during the days before presentation.","sources":[{"label":"NICE — Diabetes in children and young people","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng18/chapter/Recommendations"}]},{"category":"Endocrine & Metabolism","title":"Headache During DKA Deserves Reassessment","text":"A new or worsening headache, change in behaviour, reduced consciousness or neurological signs during DKA treatment can signal cerebral injury. Deterioration should trigger immediate senior review rather than being dismissed as ordinary dehydration discomfort.","pearl":"Monitor neurological status as carefully as glucose and electrolytes.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Diabetic ketoacidosis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Diabetic_Ketoacidosis/"}]},{"category":"Endocrine & Metabolism","title":"DKA Is a Ketone Problem, Not Just a Glucose Problem","text":"The defining physiology is insulin deficiency with ketone production and metabolic acidosis. Glucose alone cannot establish resolution; blood ketones, pH or bicarbonate and clinical status are part of the assessment.","pearl":"Track the acidosis and ketones—not only the finger-stick glucose.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Diabetic ketoacidosis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Diabetic_Ketoacidosis/"}]},{"category":"Endocrine & Metabolism","title":"Growth Velocity Can Reveal Endocrine Disease Before Height Looks Dramatic","text":"A child may remain within a broad height percentile while crossing downward over time because linear growth has slowed. Serial height measurements and growth velocity are often more informative than one isolated height.","pearl":"Plot height over time and look for the direction of travel.","sources":[{"label":"CDC — Growth charts and clinical interpretation","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/growthcharts/index.htm"}]},{"category":"Endocrine & Metabolism","title":"Pubic Hair Alone Does Not Always Mean Central Puberty","text":"Early body odour or pubic/axillary hair can result from adrenal androgen production without activation of the full hypothalamic–pituitary–gonadal axis. Breast development in girls or testicular enlargement in boys provides different information.","pearl":"Clarify which pubertal sign appeared first; not all early changes mean the same thing.","sources":[{"label":"AAP HealthyChildren — Early puberty","url":"https://www.healthychildren.org/English/ages-stages/gradeschool/puberty/Pages/When-Puberty-Starts-Early.aspx"}]},{"category":"Endocrine & Metabolism","title":"Puberty Assessment Starts With the Growth Chart","text":"The tempo of growth and pubertal progression helps distinguish normal variation from precocious or delayed puberty. Medication or household exposure to sex-steroid creams can also contribute to early signs.","pearl":"Ask about progression over months and possible hormone exposure at home.","sources":[{"label":"AAP HealthyChildren — Early puberty","url":"https://www.healthychildren.org/English/ages-stages/gradeschool/puberty/Pages/When-Puberty-Starts-Early.aspx"}]},{"category":"Endocrine & Metabolism","title":"Illness Plans Matter in Type 1 Diabetes","text":"Intercurrent illness can increase insulin needs and trigger ketosis; stopping insulin without a sick-day plan can be dangerous. Families need individualised instructions for glucose and ketone monitoring and when to seek urgent help.","pearl":"“Not eating” does not automatically mean “no insulin.” Follow the child’s diabetes sick-day plan.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Diabetic ketoacidosis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Diabetic_Ketoacidosis/"}]},{"category":"Hematology & Oncology","title":"Ferritin Can Look Normal During Inflammation","text":"Ferritin is an acute-phase reactant, so infection or inflammation can raise it and mask depleted iron stores. A normal ferritin during an inflammatory illness does not reliably exclude iron deficiency.","pearl":"Interpret ferritin alongside CRP/clinical context and the blood count.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Iron deficiency","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Iron_deficiency/"}]},{"category":"Hematology & Oncology","title":"Serum Iron Is Not the Same as Iron Stores","text":"A serum iron measurement changes with recent intake and is not a reliable stand-alone test for iron deficiency. Ferritin, red-cell indices and the clinical context are more useful for assessing iron stores.","pearl":"Do not order “iron studies” without knowing which question each test answers.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Iron deficiency","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Iron_deficiency/"}]},{"category":"Hematology & Oncology","title":"Microcytosis Has More Than One Explanation","text":"Iron deficiency is common, but thalassaemia trait and chronic inflammation can also produce microcytosis. The RBC count, red-cell distribution, ferritin, family history and response to iron help separate these possibilities.","pearl":"A low MCV is a pattern to explain—not a diagnosis by itself.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Iron deficiency","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Iron_deficiency/"}]},{"category":"Hematology & Oncology","title":"Sickle Cell Disease Makes the Spleen Functionally Disappear","text":"Repeated splenic vaso-occlusion can lead to functional asplenia, increasing susceptibility to invasive infection with encapsulated organisms. Fever in a child with sickle cell disease is therefore an urgent problem, not just another viral symptom.","pearl":"Know the child’s sickle-cell emergency plan and treat fever seriously.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Sickle cell disease","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/sickle_cell_disease_guideline/"}]},{"category":"Hematology & Oncology","title":"Pain in Sickle Cell Disease Is a Vascular Event","text":"Deoxygenated HbS polymerises, distorting red cells and obstructing small vessels. The resulting vaso-occlusion causes tissue ischaemia and severe pain; dehydration, hypoxia and infection can precipitate an episode.","pearl":"Treat pain early while assessing for infection and other complications.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Sickle cell disease","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/sickle_cell_disease_guideline/"}]},{"category":"Hematology & Oncology","title":"Chest Pain in Sickle Cell Disease Is Acute Chest Syndrome Until Assessed","text":"Acute chest syndrome can present with chest pain, hypoxaemia, respiratory symptoms and a new pulmonary infiltrate. It may follow a painful crisis and can deteriorate rapidly.","pearl":"Do not dismiss chest pain as “just another pain crisis.”","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Sickle cell disease","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/sickle_cell_disease_guideline/"}]},{"category":"Hematology & Oncology","title":"A Bigger Spleen and a Falling Haemoglobin Can Mean Sequestration","text":"Acute splenic sequestration traps blood in the spleen and can cause a rapid haemoglobin fall with splenic enlargement and circulatory compromise. Comparing the spleen and haemoglobin with the child’s baseline is crucial.","pearl":"In sickle cell disease, palpate the spleen and know the baseline.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Sickle cell disease","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/sickle_cell_disease_guideline/"}]},{"category":"Hematology & Oncology","title":"A Low Reticulocyte Count Changes the Sickle-Cell Differential","text":"An aplastic crisis, often associated with parvovirus B19, suppresses red-cell production and produces reticulocytopenia. This contrasts with many haemolytic or sequestration states where reticulocytes are increased.","pearl":"Always interpret haemoglobin together with the reticulocyte count.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Sickle cell disease","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/sickle_cell_disease_guideline/"}]},{"category":"Hematology & Oncology","title":"Fever in Sickle Cell Disease Is an Infection Emergency","text":"Functional asplenia increases the risk of overwhelming bacterial infection. Prompt clinical assessment, cultures where appropriate and empiric antibiotics according to local protocol are central to care.","pearl":"Do not wait for a child with sickle cell disease to look septic before escalating.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Sickle cell disease","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/sickle_cell_disease_guideline/"}]},{"category":"Hematology & Oncology","title":"A Toddler’s Diet Can Explain the Blood Count","text":"Excess cow’s milk, limited iron-rich foods and rapid growth can create a high-risk nutritional pattern for iron deficiency. Dietary history often explains why a child has microcytosis before an extensive work-up is started.","pearl":"Ask what the child drinks and eats across a typical day.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Iron deficiency","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Iron_deficiency/"}]},{"category":"Hematology & Oncology","title":"Stroke Is a Major Sickle-Cell Complication Even in Childhood","text":"Children with sickle cell disease can develop acute stroke; sudden weakness, facial asymmetry, speech change, severe headache or altered consciousness requires emergency stroke assessment.","pearl":"Any new focal neurological deficit in sickle cell disease is an emergency.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Sickle cell disease","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/sickle_cell_disease_guideline/"}]},{"category":"Hematology & Oncology","title":"Dehydration Is a Modifiable Trigger for Vaso-Occlusion","text":"Low fluid intake and fluid losses can contribute to sickling by increasing blood viscosity and promoting vaso-occlusion. Hydration advice is part of prevention, though acute fluid treatment must still be individualised.","pearl":"Ask about vomiting, diarrhoea, heat exposure and drinking before a pain episode.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Sickle cell disease","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/sickle_cell_disease_guideline/"}]},{"category":"Hematology & Oncology","title":"Iron Deficiency Can Exist Before Anaemia Appears","text":"Iron stores may be depleted before haemoglobin falls below the anaemia threshold. Early deficiency can still affect attention, behaviour and development, which is why ferritin and dietary risk matter.","pearl":"A normal haemoglobin does not guarantee normal iron stores.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Iron deficiency","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Iron_deficiency/"}]},{"category":"Hematology & Oncology","title":"The Dose of Iron Means Elemental Iron","text":"When iron is prescribed, the clinically relevant amount is elemental iron, not the total weight of the iron salt or liquid preparation. Confusing the two can lead to underdosing or overdosing.","pearl":"Check the elemental-iron concentration on the actual product label.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Iron deficiency","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Iron_deficiency/"}]},{"category":"Hematology & Oncology","title":"Sickle-Cell Pain Can Be Abdominal, Not Just Bone Pain","text":"Vaso-occlusive pain can affect the abdomen and may mimic a surgical abdomen. However, a child with sickle cell disease can also develop true appendicitis or another surgical problem.","pearl":"Do not anchor: treat sickle pain and still investigate new abdominal red flags.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Sickle cell disease","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/sickle_cell_disease_guideline/"}]},{"category":"Growth, Development & Adolescence","title":"A Percentile Is a Position, Not a Grade","text":"A growth percentile tells you how a measurement compares with a reference population; it does not by itself diagnose health or disease. A child at a low percentile may be healthy if growth is steady and family pattern fits.","pearl":"Look at the curve and the child—not just the percentile label.","sources":[{"label":"CDC — Growth charts and clinical interpretation","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/growthcharts/index.htm"}]},{"category":"Growth, Development & Adolescence","title":"Crossing Percentile Lines Can Matter More Than Being Small","text":"A child who consistently tracks a lower centile may be healthy, while a child who drops across centile channels may need evaluation. Accurate measurements and plotting over time are essential to interpret the pattern.","pearl":"The direction of the curve often tells more than the current point.","sources":[{"label":"CDC — Growth charts and clinical interpretation","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/growthcharts/index.htm"}]},{"category":"Growth, Development & Adolescence","title":"Use the Right Growth Standard for the Child’s Age","text":"WHO growth standards are used for children under two years in CDC guidance, with CDC charts used from age two onward in the US system. The chart reference changes the interpretation, so do not switch systems casually.","pearl":"Document which growth standard you used when interpreting a measurement.","sources":[{"label":"CDC — Growth charts and clinical interpretation","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/growthcharts/index.htm"}]},{"category":"Growth, Development & Adolescence","title":"Growth Charts Are Not a Stand-Alone Diagnosis","text":"Growth charts contribute to an overall clinical picture; they are not intended to be used as the sole diagnostic instrument. Nutrition, family heights, chronic disease, pubertal stage and measurement quality all matter.","pearl":"A percentile is a prompt for clinical context, not a verdict.","sources":[{"label":"CDC — Growth charts and clinical interpretation","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/growthcharts/index.htm"}]},{"category":"Growth, Development & Adolescence","title":"Autism Screening Has Specific Windows","text":"AAP-referenced CDC guidance recommends general developmental screening at 9, 18 and 30 months and autism-specific screening at 18 and 24 months. Screening identifies children who need further assessment; it is not the diagnosis itself.","pearl":"Put developmental screening into the visit schedule instead of relying on memory.","sources":[{"label":"CDC — Autism screening for healthcare providers","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/autism/hcp/diagnosis/screening.html"}]},{"category":"Growth, Development & Adolescence","title":"A Screening Score Is Not the Same as a Diagnosis","text":"A positive autism screen indicates that a child needs a more complete developmental evaluation. A negative screen does not override persistent caregiver concern or observed developmental differences.","pearl":"Treat screening as a doorway to assessment, not a final label.","sources":[{"label":"CDC — Autism screening for healthcare providers","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/autism/hcp/diagnosis/screening.html"}]},{"category":"Growth, Development & Adolescence","title":"Developmental Regression Is More Concerning Than a Slow Milestone","text":"A child who loses words, social engagement, motor skills or other abilities previously acquired needs prompt assessment. Regression is not simply a child being “late”; it suggests a change in function that must be explained.","pearl":"Always ask: what could the child do before that they can no longer do now?","sources":[{"label":"CDC — Autism screening for healthcare providers","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/autism/hcp/diagnosis/screening.html"}]},{"category":"Growth, Development & Adolescence","title":"A Well-Child Visit Is a Screening Opportunity, Not Just a Vaccine Visit","text":"Preventive visits bring together growth, development, vision, hearing, mental health, risk assessment and anticipatory guidance. Many clinically important problems are found before a family comes in with a symptom.","pearl":"Use routine visits to look for what has not yet become a complaint.","sources":[{"label":"AAP — Bright Futures preventive services","url":"https://www.aap.org/en/practice-management/bright-futures/bright-futures-quality-improvement/bright-futures-preventive-services-quality-improvement-measures/"}]},{"category":"Growth, Development & Adolescence","title":"Adolescent Care Needs Private Conversation Time","text":"Adolescents may disclose sensitive concerns more honestly when given developmentally appropriate time to speak without a caregiver in the room, while confidentiality limits and safety exceptions are explained clearly.","pearl":"Make private time a routine part of adolescent visits, not a sign that something is wrong.","sources":[{"label":"AAP — Bright Futures preventive services","url":"https://www.aap.org/en/practice-management/bright-futures/bright-futures-quality-improvement/bright-futures-preventive-services-quality-improvement-measures/"}]},{"category":"Growth, Development & Adolescence","title":"Pubertal Timing Is a Trajectory, Not a Single Birthday","text":"The age at onset, sequence and speed of pubertal progression all matter. A single early sign can be benign, while rapid progression or multiple pubertal changes may warrant evaluation.","pearl":"Record stage and progression over time instead of relying on a vague “early/late” label.","sources":[{"label":"AAP HealthyChildren — Early puberty","url":"https://www.healthychildren.org/English/ages-stages/gradeschool/puberty/Pages/When-Puberty-Starts-Early.aspx"}]},{"category":"Skin, Allergy & Multisystem Clues","title":"Anaphylaxis Is a Clinical Diagnosis—There Is No Time to Wait for a Lab","text":"Most anaphylaxis cases are recognised from the sudden pattern of symptoms after a likely exposure. Laboratory tests are not required before emergency treatment, and normal skin findings do not exclude the diagnosis.","pearl":"Treat the clinical emergency first; tests must not delay adrenaline.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Anaphylaxis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Anaphylaxis/"}]},{"category":"Skin, Allergy & Multisystem Clues","title":"Skin Symptoms Alone Are Not the Same as Anaphylaxis","text":"Hives or facial swelling without airway, breathing or circulatory compromise do not automatically meet the clinical picture of anaphylaxis. Conversely, anaphylaxis can occur without skin signs.","pearl":"Look for organ-system compromise and timing after exposure.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Anaphylaxis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Anaphylaxis/"}]},{"category":"Skin, Allergy & Multisystem Clues","title":"Eczema Is Usually a Clinical Diagnosis","text":"Typical atopic eczema is recognised from the history and skin examination; routine laboratory testing is not usually needed. Tests become relevant when the course is atypical, infection is suspected or treatment-resistant disease raises another diagnosis.","pearl":"Do not turn every itchy rash into an allergy panel.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Eczema","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/eczema/"}]},{"category":"Skin, Allergy & Multisystem Clues","title":"Moisturiser Is Daily Treatment, Not Just a Flare Treatment","text":"Regular generous use of a plain emollient supports the skin barrier and reduces dryness between flares. Families often need demonstration and a written plan because correct application is part of effective eczema care.","pearl":"Ask what is applied on ordinary days, not only during severe flares.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Eczema","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/eczema/"}]},{"category":"Skin, Allergy & Multisystem Clues","title":"Eczema That Suddenly Becomes Painful and Vesicular Needs Urgent Review","text":"Eczema herpeticum is a potentially serious viral infection that can complicate atopic dermatitis. A rapid change to painful, clustered or punched-out lesions, fever or a child who appears unwell should prompt urgent assessment.","pearl":"A sudden painful change in chronic eczema is not just a “bad flare.”","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Eczema","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/eczema/"}]},{"category":"Skin, Allergy & Multisystem Clues","title":"The Glass Test Helps Identify a Non-Blanching Rash","text":"Petechiae and purpura do not disappear under pressure, unlike many common blanching rashes. A glass can help demonstrate this, but the test does not identify the cause or replace urgent clinical assessment when the child is unwell.","pearl":"The glass test describes the rash; it does not diagnose meningococcal disease.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Petechiae and purpura","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Fever_and_Petechiae_Purpura/"}]},{"category":"Skin, Allergy & Multisystem Clues","title":"A Rash Can Change While You Are Watching","text":"A rash associated with meningococcal disease may progress from blanching to non-blanching. Reassessing a changing child and rash is important; one early skin examination can become outdated.","pearl":"Repeat the skin examination if the child deteriorates or the story changes.","sources":[{"label":"NICE — Meningococcal disease: recognition","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng240"}]},{"category":"Skin, Allergy & Multisystem Clues","title":"Palpable Purpura on the Legs Can Be a Systemic Clue","text":"IgA vasculitis classically produces palpable purpura, often on dependent areas such as the buttocks and lower limbs, and can be accompanied by joint, gastrointestinal and renal findings.","pearl":"When you see palpable purpura, check joints, abdomen, blood pressure and urine.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — IgA vasculitis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/henochSchonlein_Purpura/"}]},{"category":"Skin, Allergy & Multisystem Clues","title":"A Rash With Abdominal Pain May Be a Vasculitis","text":"In IgA vasculitis, bowel-vessel inflammation can cause abdominal pain and, rarely, bleeding, ischaemia or intussusception. Severe or escalating pain needs urgent review rather than reassurance based on the visible rash.","pearl":"The skin may be the easiest organ to see, not the only organ involved.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — IgA vasculitis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/henochSchonlein_Purpura/"}]},{"category":"Skin, Allergy & Multisystem Clues","title":"Red Eyes Without Discharge Can Be a Kawasaki Clue","text":"Bilateral non-purulent conjunctival injection is one of the mucocutaneous features of Kawasaki disease. When paired with prolonged fever and oral, extremity, lymph-node or rash changes, it should raise suspicion.","pearl":"Ask whether the eyes are red without the sticky discharge of conjunctivitis.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Kawasaki disease","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Kawasaki_disease_guideline/"}]},{"category":"Skin, Allergy & Multisystem Clues","title":"Eczema Can Be a Marker of Sleep and Quality-of-Life Burden","text":"Itch can fragment sleep, affect school, and burden the whole family even when the rash does not look dramatic in clinic. Ask about night waking and daily function when assessing severity.","pearl":"Measure eczema impact by sleep and function as well as skin surface area.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Eczema","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/eczema/"}]},{"category":"Skin, Allergy & Multisystem Clues","title":"Petechiae Need Context—Not Every Case Is Meningococcal Disease","text":"Most children with petechiae do not have meningococcal disease, but an ill child with a non-blanching or rapidly spreading rash requires urgent assessment. The danger comes from missing the sick child, not from the rash label alone.","pearl":"Triage the whole child; do not panic at every dot or dismiss the unwell child.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Petechiae and purpura","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Fever_and_Petechiae_Purpura/"}]},{"category":"Skin, Allergy & Multisystem Clues","title":"Respiratory Symptoms May Be the Main Feature of Food Anaphylaxis","text":"In children, respiratory features can predominate in anaphylaxis, while gastrointestinal and skin manifestations vary. A sudden cough, wheeze, hoarse voice or breathing difficulty after a likely exposure must be taken seriously.","pearl":"Do not require a rash plus wheeze before recognising the pattern.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — Anaphylaxis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Anaphylaxis/"}]},{"category":"Skin, Allergy & Multisystem Clues","title":"Scrotal Pain Can Occur in IgA Vasculitis—but Torsion Must Be Excluded","text":"IgA vasculitis can cause scrotal oedema, pain or haemorrhage, yet testicular torsion remains a time-critical alternative diagnosis. A known vasculitis diagnosis must not short-circuit emergency examination.","pearl":"A child with acute testicular pain needs urgent torsion assessment regardless of the rash history.","sources":[{"label":"RCH — IgA vasculitis","url":"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/henochSchonlein_Purpura/"}]},{"category":"Skin, Allergy & Multisystem Clues","title":"Ill Appearance Can Be Harder to Read on Darker Skin","text":"Pallor, mottling, cyanosis and rash changes can be less obvious across different skin tones. Examine appropriate sites, including conjunctivae and mucous membranes, and rely on behaviour, perfusion and vital signs as well.","pearl":"Use the whole examination; do not let skin tone reduce clinical suspicion.","sources":[{"label":"NICE — Meningococcal disease: recognition","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng240"}]}];